<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_note"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Note: </span>Corresponding author. E-Mail <a href="mailto:M.Pereyra@hw.ac.uk" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">M.Pereyra@hw.ac.uk</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) methods are fundamental to Bayesian computation, but can be computationally intensive, especially in high-dimensional settings. Push-forward generative models, such as generative adversarial networks (GANs), variational auto-encoders and normalising flows offer a computationally efficient alternative for posterior sampling. However, push-forward models are opaque as they lack the modularity of Bayes Theorem, leading to poor generalisation with respect to changes in the likelihood function. In this work, we introduce a novel approach to GAN architecture design by applying deep unfolding to Langevin MCMC algorithms. This paradigm maps fixed-step iterative algorithms onto modular neural networks, yielding architectures that are both flexible and amenable to interpretation. Crucially, our design allows key model parameters to be specified at inference time, offering robustness to changes in the likelihood parameters. We train these unfolded samplers end-to-end using a supervised regularized Wasserstein GAN framework for posterior sampling. Through extensive Bayesian imaging experiments, we demonstrate that our proposed approach achieves high sampling accuracy and excellent computational efficiency, while retaining the physics consistency, adaptability and interpretability of classical MCMC strategies.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.2.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
    
<p id="abstract1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Keywords:</span> Bayesian statistics, inverse problems, deep unfolding, generative adversarial networks, Markov Chain Monte Carlo, computational imaging,</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We consider the inverse problem of reconstructing an <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>-valued signal <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> given an <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d_{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d_{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>-valued measurement <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> with a likelihood function</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S1.E1X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E1X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E1X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim p(y|x^{\star})\propto\exp(-f_{y}(x^{\star}))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim p(y|x^{\star})\propto\exp(-f_{y}(x^{\star})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where the negative log-likelihood <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{y}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{y}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is convex for all <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. As a canonical example, we consider the linear Gaussian problem</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S1.E2X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E2X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y=Ax^{\star}+\epsilon,\qquad\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{y}^{2}I_{d_{y}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>y</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>I</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y=Ax^{\star}+\epsilon,\qquad\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{y}^{2}I_{d_{y}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">with likelihood <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y|x)=\mathcal{N}(y;Ax,\sigma_{y}^{2}I_{d_{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y|x)=\mathcal{N}(y;Ax,\sigma_{y}^{2}I_{d_{y}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A:\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}\to\mathbb{R}^{{d_{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A:\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}\to\mathbb{R}^{{d_{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear degradation operator and <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> controls the scale of measurement noise. Such problems arise in multiple application areas including compressive sensing, tomography <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> and in computational imaging, with applications to scientific, medical and natural images <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>. The methods studied in this paper are intended for the general class of problems taking the form (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), including but not limited to those mentioned above.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The inverse problem (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is typically ill-conditioned. For example, in the canonical Gaussian example, <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is often low-rank or has a high conditioning number. To obtain accurate reconstructions, we therefore require additional regularity constraints on the space of solutions. Within a statistical framework, we assume <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is a realisation of the random variable <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\sim p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\sim p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for an (unknown) distribution <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> defined on <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Bayes Theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>, the distribution of signals <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, given a measurement <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is expressed through the posterior distribution</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x|y)=\frac{p(y|x)p(x)}{\int_{\mathbb{R}^{dx}}p(y|x)p(x)\text{d}x}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x|y)=\frac{p(y|x)p(x)}{\int_{\mathbb{R}^{dx}}p(y|x)p(x)\text{d}x}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, the exact distribution of <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> is unknown, precluding direct inference using <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x|y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x|y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Instead, we approximate <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> by a parametric prior distribution <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(x)=\exp(-g_{\theta}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(x)=\exp(-g_{\theta}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\theta}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\theta}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the parameter(s) <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> which are tuned depending on the problem. For ill-posed inverse problems, a choice of prior which is accurately able to describe the underlying data distribution of <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> is crucial.
Traditionally, the <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> would be designed to promote solutions with desired or expected regularity properties such as smoothness. Modern approaches for modelling prior knowledge are predominantly data-driven and use a deep neural-network with high-dimensional weights <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> learned to fit an empirical data distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Given a chosen parametrization of the prior, we consider the associated posterior distribution</p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(x|y)=\frac{p(y|x)p_{\theta}(x)}{\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}}p(y|x)p_{\theta}(x)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msup></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(x|y)=\frac{p(y|x)p_{\theta}(x)}{\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}}p(y|x)p_{\theta}(x)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The proportionality constant in (<a href="#S1.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and properties of the posterior distribution (mean, variance) are typically unknown, necessitating numerical integration. Techniques from optimization can be used to maximise the posterior density, providing a point estimate <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{MAP}}=\argmax_{x}\pi_{\theta}(x|y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>MAP</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{MAP}}=\argmax_{x}\pi_{\theta}(x|y)</annotation></semantics></math>. To obtain higher order statistical information, Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) techniques are often utilised to sample a Markov Chain ergodic with respect to <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(x|y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(x|y)</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-step transition kernel of the Markov Chain converges to the posterior distribution. Approximate samples from (<a href="#S1.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) can therefore be obtained from long-time integration of the Markov chain, after discarding a suitable burn-in period. These samples can then be used, for example, to inform statistical decision-making about the distribution of <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> given the observation <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}=y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}=y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Typically, the weights <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> are learned during an offline phase, following which a chosen numerical scheme is employed to sample <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(x|y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(x|y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of the likelihood model <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math>. In the machine learning community, such methods are referred to as zero-shot: the task of reconstructing <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> given the observation <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is performed without specialising the model for solving the inverse problem (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) during an offline training phase. Alternatively, one can consider training <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> specifically with the likelihood <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math> and the chosen numerical scheme to optimise the exploration of the posterior (<a href="#S1.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">MCMC with a data-driven prior provides a modular, transparent approach to Bayesian inverse problem reconstruction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>. Such methods have been extensively studied in the imaging literature in recent years, particularly in combination with score-based generative diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. However, such approaches often rely on a large number of numerical integrations approximating an underlying continuous-time process (for example Langevin or reversed-SDE), resulting in accurate but slow algorithms. Black-box methods for sampling <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(x|y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(x|y)</annotation></semantics></math> include conditional GANs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, which train a deep neural-network adversarially end-to-end to transport <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> into samples from <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(x|y)\approx p(x|y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(x|y)\approx p(x|y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Black-box models can be both accurate and fast, but come at the expense of interpretability lacking a natural embedding of the likelihood model <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math> in the reconstruction process, making it difficult to account for small perturbations in the likelihood model at inference time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we propose a method of combining data-driven MCMC and conditional GANs. Specifically, we propose a novel framework for unfolding <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> iterations of a truncated MCMC chain designed to target the true posterior <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x|y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x|y)</annotation></semantics></math>. The proposed approach builds upon the unfolding of deterministic optimization schemes, a technique which jointly trains <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and hyper-parameters from <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-iterations of a truncated numerical optimization scheme as a <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer recurrent neural network, is considered in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> (see <a href="#S2.F1.sf1" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 2.1 Setup and notation ‣ 2 Background, Notation and Related Works ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a> and <a href="#S2" title="2 Background, Notation and Related Works ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). The proposed architecture is illustrated in <a href="#S2.F1.sf2" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 2.1 Setup and notation ‣ 2 Background, Notation and Related Works ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a>. The unfolded MCMC model provides high-order statistical information in contrast to optimization methods. This information is crucial, for example, in providing uncertainty estimation on model outputs. Leveraging the modularity and interpretability of Markov transition kernels, the proposed deep unfolded generative networks strike a careful balance between performance and explainability. Moreover, the posterior (<a href="#S1.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) contains a natural embedding of the likelihood. Thus, an unfolded MCMC kernel targeting (<a href="#S1.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) provides a natural embedding of the likelihood function, which is often lacking in black-box neural-network architectures trained for end-to-end reconstruction. A recent and related work which considers the unfolding of a stochastic denoising diffusion sampler is <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The paper is structured as follows: <a href="#S2" title="2 Background, Notation and Related Works ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> discusses background on Bayesian and data-driven methods for ill-posed inverse problems with a discussion of related literature. The proposed unrolled MCMC architecture and a training procedure tailored to such neural networks is discussed in <a href="#S3" title="3 Proposed Methodology ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. In <a href="#S4" title="4 Numerical Experiments ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we benchmark unfolded MCMC architectures on image deblurring and radio interferometry problems. These problems illustrate two distinct forms of challenging inversion: the deblurring operator is severely ill-conditioned, whereas radio interferometry is ill-posed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The key contributions of this work are summarised as follows.</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A formal framework is proposed for general unfolded MCMC algorithms for posterior sampling. By considering a broad class of algorithms, we intend the methodology to be beneficial to a wide range of inverse problems.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a novel training scheme for GANs arising from unfolded MCMC kernels in (<a href="#S3.E17" title="Equation 17 ‣ 3.1 Architecture ‣ 3 Proposed Methodology ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>). To promote accurate sampling of posterior distributions, our approach is inspired by recent developments in conditional adversarial networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, distilled diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> and unrolled optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A systematic empirical study of unrolled MCMC architectures for two problems: a comprehensive ablation study on the MNIST dataset to afford accurate comparison of posterior distributions; and a high-dimensional problem in radio interferometry, with gridded visibilities simulated visibility patterns from the MeerKAT radio telescope.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background, Notation and Related Works</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section introduces key notation and summarises key background information on techniques for computational Bayesian inverse problems. In what follows, we assume the existence of a set of empirical data <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=\{x^{(n)}\}_{n=1}^{N_{\text{data}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=\{x^{(n)}\}_{n=1}^{N_{\text{data}}}</annotation></semantics></math> of samples from the intractable prior <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Crucially, this allows one to consider data-driven parametrisations <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> leveraging techniques from machine learning.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Setup and notation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the joint law of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}},\ {\mathbf{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}},\ {\mathbf{y}})</annotation></semantics></math>, the law of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the law of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Given measurable spaces <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Omega_{1},\Sigma_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Omega_{1},\Sigma_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Omega_{2},\Sigma_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Omega_{2},\Sigma_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, a measurable function <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\Omega_{1}\to\Omega_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\Omega_{1}\to\Omega_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and a measure <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu:\Sigma_{1}\to\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu:\Sigma_{1}\to\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote the push-forward measure <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\#}\mu:\Omega_{2}\to\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\#}\mu:\Omega_{2}\to\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. For a random variable <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{w}}\sim p_{\mathbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{w}}\sim p_{\mathbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> taking values in a Banach space <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and measurable function <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{w}}\sim p_{\mathbf{w}}}[f({\mathbf{w}})]=\int_{E}f({\mathbf{w}})\text{d}p_{\mathbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>E</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{w}}\sim p_{\mathbf{w}}}[f({\mathbf{w}})]=\int_{E}f({\mathbf{w}})\text{d}p_{\mathbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>. When the random variable to be integrated is obvious from context, we simplify the notation to <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[f({\mathbf{w}})]=\int_{E}f({\mathbf{w}})\text{d}p_{\mathbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>E</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>d</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[f({\mathbf{w}})]=\int_{E}f({\mathbf{w}})\text{d}p_{\mathbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For a convex function <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the proximal operator of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> as the function <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\prox_{g}(x)=\argmin_{z}g(z)+\|x-z\|^{2}/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\prox_{g}(x)=\argmin_{z}g(z)+\|x-z\|^{2}/2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a (deep) artificial neural network <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{NN}_{\theta}:y_{\text{in}}\mapsto x_{\text{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>NN</mtext><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{NN}_{\theta}:y_{\text{in}}\mapsto x_{\text{out}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with weights <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, by a neural function evaluation (NFE) we refer to a single call <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{NN}_{\theta}(x_{\text{in}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>NN</mtext><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{NN}_{\theta}(x_{\text{in}})</annotation></semantics></math>. For a parametrised posterior distribution, we denote a random variable with distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(x|y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(x|y)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The joint law of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}},{\mathbf{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}},{\mathbf{y}})</annotation></semantics></math> is then denoted <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}},{\mathbf{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}},{\mathbf{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
