</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 433.02 -137.85)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.99em;--ltx-fo-height:4.99em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="69" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 69)" width="69"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><img src="2602.20758v1/figures/RI/visualizations_8/LAT_probes_4h/std_recon_4.png" id="S4.F4.pic1.g555" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:69/69;" width="69" height="69" alt="Refer to caption"></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 442.52 -159.78)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.61em;--ltx-fo-height:1.45em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="20" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 20)" width="50"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><img src="2602.20758v1/x8.png" id="S4.F4.pic1.g556" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="width:5.019em; height:2.007em;aspect-ratio:83/35;" width="83" height="35" alt="Refer to caption"></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 434.4 -79.15)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.11em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="29.21"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.F4.pic1.243" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">0.31
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 433.02 -241.19)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.99em;--ltx-fo-height:4.99em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="69" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 69)" width="69"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><img src="2602.20758v1/figures/RI/visualizations_8/LAT_probes_4h/err_4.png" id="S4.F4.pic1.g557" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:69/69;" width="69" height="69" alt="Refer to caption"></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 442.52 -263.13)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.61em;--ltx-fo-height:1.45em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="20" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 20)" width="50"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><img src="2602.20758v1/x9.png" id="S4.F4.pic1.g558" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="width:5.019em; height:2.007em;aspect-ratio:83/35;" width="83" height="35" alt="Refer to caption"></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g><g></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FF0000;" stroke="#FF0000"><path style="fill:none" d="M 514.04 -184.31 C 514.04 -176.7 507.87 -170.53 500.26 -170.53 C 492.65 -170.53 486.48 -176.7 486.48 -184.31 C 486.48 -191.92 492.65 -198.09 500.26 -198.09 C 507.87 -198.09 514.04 -191.92 514.04 -184.31 Z M 500.26 -184.31"></path></g></g></g></g></g><g></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FF0000;" stroke="#FF0000"><path style="fill:none" d="M 18.96 -80.97 C 18.96 -73.36 12.79 -67.19 5.18 -67.19 C -2.43 -67.19 -8.6 -73.36 -8.6 -80.97 C -8.6 -88.58 -2.43 -94.75 5.18 -94.75 C 12.79 -94.75 18.96 -88.58 18.96 -80.97 Z M 5.18 -80.97"></path></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Qualitative comparison of example reconstructions on ground-truth images from the PROBES dataset. The left column displays the ground truth, the pseudo-inverse <math id="S4.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\dagger}y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\dagger}y</annotation></semantics></math>, and the sampled mask <math id="S4.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> applied in the Fourier domain. We compare the posterior mean along, the residual and predicted residual (approximated via the standard dev. of 8 posterior samples) on a <math id="S4.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 32</annotation></semantics></math> scale. In the upper-left corner of each predicted residual, we report the Pearson correlation to the true residual.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we introduced a novel framework for constructing and training deep posterior samplers by unfolding <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> iterations of a desired Markov kernel. This provides a modular and interpretable framework for constructing conditional generative networks. At the expense of an offline training phase over a constrained class of inverse problems, unfolded MCMC architectures can fine-tune weights in a parametrised prior distribution and the Markov transition kernel to attain superior sampling accuracy and computational efficiency over traditional (zero-shot) MCMC methods. The methodology in <a href="#S3" title="3 Proposed Methodology ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> applies generally to a broad class of MCMC transition kernels and inverse problems. We illustrate the efficiency of unfolded algorithms in two settings: an unfolded split Gibbs sampler for deblurring handwritten digits and an unfolded Langevin sampler with a score-matching prior applied to radio interferometry. Future directions of this research involve adapting this methodology to the pruning and distillation of conditional score-based models to support accurate posterior sampling with the efficiency of generative adversarial networks. A key assumption in this work is that models are trained with access to ground-truth data. An important direction of future research is to adapt the methodology to train models in a self-supervised manner, bypassing the need for ground-truth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Acknowledgements</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work was supported by UKRI Engineering and Physical Sciences Research
Council (EPSRC) (EP/V006134/1, EP/Z534481/1). We acknowledge the use of the Heriot-Watt University high-performance
computing facility (DMOG) and associated support services in the completion of this work.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Implementation Details</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Sliced Wasserstein</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The sliced Wasserstein metric between <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}},{\mathbf{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}},{\mathbf{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as</p>
<table id="A1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SW}(p_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{y}}},p_{{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}},{\mathbf{y}}})=\mathbb{E}_{\pi\sim\mathcal{S}(d_{x}\times{d_{y}})}[\mathcal{W}_{2}(\pi_{\#}p_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{y}}},\pi_{\#}p_{{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}},{\mathbf{y}}})]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>SW</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SW}(p_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{y}}},p_{{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}},{\mathbf{y}}})=\mathbb{E}_{\pi\sim\mathcal{S}(d_{x}\times{d_{y}})}[\mathcal{W}_{2}(\pi_{\#}p_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{y}}},\pi_{\#}p_{{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}},{\mathbf{y}}})],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(d_{x}\times{d_{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(d_{x}\times{d_{y}})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a uniform measure on the unit sphere embedded in <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}\times\mathbb{R}^{{d_{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}\times\mathbb{R}^{{d_{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We make two approximations to estimate the SW distance: firstly, for the outer expectation by using Monte Carlo approximation over 1,000 independent samples from <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(d_{x}\times{d_{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(d_{x}\times{d_{y}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Secondly, for each model posterior <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(x|y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(x|y)</annotation></semantics></math>, we replace <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}},{\mathbf{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}},{\mathbf{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with empirical approximations each using <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5,000</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint sets of independent samples from the 10,000 test images. Approximating the <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> distance between the empirical distributions instead induces a bias. We estimate the degree of bias induced by computing the SW between two empirical measures formed of independent samples sets from <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> of size 5000. Over 10 repetitions, the baseline SW was found to be <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(5.57\pm 0.51)\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>5.57</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.51</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(5.57\pm 0.51)\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>. As SW scores approach this value, random perturbations make similar models indistinguishable using this metric.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Frechet Inception Distance with VAE Embedding</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Fréchet Inception Distance (FID) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> between <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> computes the Fréchet distance between the two measures under an inception embedding <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FID}\big(p_{{\mathbf{x}}},p_{{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}}}\big)=\mathcal{W}_{2}(\mathcal{E}_{\#}p_{\mathbf{x}},\mathcal{E}_{\#}p_{{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>FID</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msubsup></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msubsup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FID}\big(p_{{\mathbf{x}}},p_{{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}}}\big)=\mathcal{W}_{2}(\mathcal{E}_{\#}p_{\mathbf{x}},\mathcal{E}_{\#}p_{{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The CFID <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> instead computes the expected FID of posterior measures over all realisations of <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CFID}(p_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{y}}},p_{{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}},{\mathbf{y}}})=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{y}}\sim p_{\mathbf{y}}}[\mathcal{W}_{2}(\mathcal{E}_{\#}p_{{\mathbf{x}}|{\mathbf{y}}},\mathcal{E}_{\#}p_{{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}}|{\mathbf{y}}})]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>CFID</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msubsup><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CFID}(p_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{y}}},p_{{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}},{\mathbf{y}}})=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{y}}\sim p_{\mathbf{y}}}[\mathcal{W}_{2}(\mathcal{E}_{\#}p_{{\mathbf{x}}|{\mathbf{y}}},\mathcal{E}_{\#}p_{{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}^{\mathbf{y}}|{\mathbf{y}}})].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Typically, the FID is computed using either the Inception-V3 or VGG encoder <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> and uses an implicit assumption that the push-forward <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\#}p_{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\#}p_{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> is approximately Gaussian. The mean and covariance of the embeddings are approximated by samples from the test dataset. The Fréchet distance between the two Gaussian approximations can then be computed exactly. Due to limitations in the Inception-V3 and VGG encoders at embedding MNIST data into a Gaussian distribution, we follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, Appendix D.1]</cite> and use <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}=E_{\theta}^{\text{VAE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>θ</mi><mtext>VAE</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}=E_{\theta}^{\text{VAE}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the pre-trained VAE-encoder from the VAE-SGS method discussed in <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Image Deblurring on MNIST ‣ 4 Numerical Experiments ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>. Since this encoder is trained explicitly to minimise the Kullbach-Leibler divergence between <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E_{\theta}^{\text{VAE}})_{\#}p_{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>θ</mi><mtext>VAE</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E_{\theta}^{\text{VAE}})_{\#}p_{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> and a Gaussian measure, the resulting (C)FID scores are informative. We refer to the CFID metric with <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}=E_{\theta}^{\text{VAE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>θ</mi><mtext>VAE</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}=E_{\theta}^{\text{VAE}}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{2}^{\text{Latent}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn><mtext>Latent</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{2}^{\text{Latent}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Image Deblurring on MNIST ‣ 4 Numerical Experiments ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Additional Experimental Results</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present here additional numerical results to highlight further properties of the unfolded models designed in <a href="#S4" title="4 Numerical Experiments ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. A particular emphasis is placed on exploring the adaptability of unfolded architectures to small perturbations in the observation model; a key motivation for using physics-inspired unfolded architectures.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Out-of-Distribution Performance on MNIST</h3>

