</figure>
<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The U-SGS model trained in <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Image Deblurring on MNIST ‣ 4 Numerical Experiments ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> contains a natural embedding through proximal gradient steps of the forward operator <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, the trained model was able to well approximate the task of motion deblurring where <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> was sampled randomly from a class of motion blur kernels. In this section, we present numerical results which evaluate the performance of the pre-trained models on out-of-training-distribution inverse problems by randomly sampling the motion blur kernel using a different generating distribution <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{k}}\sim\mathcal{GP}(19,\lambda_{\text{Matern}}=0.5,\sigma_{\text{Matern}}=0.4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐤</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>19</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>Matern</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>Matern</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{k}}\sim\mathcal{GP}(19,\lambda_{\text{Matern}}=0.5,\sigma_{\text{Matern}}=0.4)</annotation></semantics></math> during the test loop. Kernels from this distribution cover a larger receptive field with greater variability. We present quantitative results in <a href="#A2.T3" title="In B.1 Out-of-Distribution Performance on MNIST ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. All methods report worse performance than in <a href="#S4.T1" title="In 4.1.5 Results ‣ 4.1 Image Deblurring on MNIST ‣ 4 Numerical Experiments ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, reflecting the more challenging forward operation. However, the difference in performance between each U-SGS model and RCGAN is greater than the experiment on in-distribution inverse problems. This can be observed qualitatively in <a href="#A2.F5" title="In B.1 Out-of-Distribution Performance on MNIST ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> where the artefacts arising in samples from the black-box RCGAN due to the random forward operators are amplified for the out-of-distribution problem. In contrast, U-SGS and the zero-shot VAE-SGS methods both generate more realistic samples from the MNIST dataset.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Additional Results on PROBES data</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this supplement, we provide further qualitative and quantitative results for the RI reconstructions discussed in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Radio Interferometry ‣ 4 Numerical Experiments ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.2.1 </span>Residuals at Higher Resolution</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To compare the ability of each model to quantify uncertainties at different scales, in <a href="#A2.F6" title="In B.2.1 Residuals at Higher Resolution ‣ B.2 Additional Results on PROBES data ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> we display the reconstructions in <a href="#S4.F4" title="In 4.2.6 Results ‣ 4.2 Radio Interferometry ‣ 4 Numerical Experiments ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> with additional residuals at a scale of <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64\times 64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times 64</annotation></semantics></math> pixels. Qualitative results on a different image from the test set can be found in <a href="#A2.F6" title="In B.2.1 Residuals at Higher Resolution ‣ B.2 Additional Results on PROBES data ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. At the higher resolution, LATINO and IRIS (64) are again unable to capture and quantify uncertainty around finer structures in the ground-truth image, with U-LATINO displaying errors focused on directions aligned with the ground truth image and samples which have a standard deviation highly correlated to the residuals.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F6" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A2.F6.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="545" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 635.83 545" width="635.83"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" color="#000000" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1" stroke="#000000" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="butt" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-opacity="1" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,545) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(121.24,0) translate(0,469.83)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -62.06 -137.85)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.99em;--ltx-fo-height:4.99em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="69" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 69)" width="69"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><img src="2602.20758v1/figures/RI/visualizations/ULAT_probes_4h/ground_truth_4.png" id="A2.F6.pic1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:69/69;" width="69" height="69" alt="Refer to caption"></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -52.56 -159.78)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.61em;--ltx-fo-height:1.45em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="20" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 20)" width="50"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><img src="2602.20758v1/colorbar_ground_truth_4.png" id="A2.F6.pic1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="width:5.019em; height:2.007em;aspect-ratio:83/35;" width="83" height="35" alt="Refer to caption"></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1