<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Out-of-Distribution Masks for Radio Interferometry</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Out-of-Distribution Performance on MNIST ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>, we observed that unfolded networks extend naturally to small perturbations in the observation process. We test a similar setup for the RI task here. In particular, we consider the same forward observation mode (<a href="#S4.E23" title="Equation 23 ‣ 4.2.1 Task ‣ 4.2 Radio Interferometry ‣ 4 Numerical Experiments ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) as in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Radio Interferometry ‣ 4 Numerical Experiments ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> but for each observation generating a mask <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{m}}\sim\mathcal{U}(\mathcal{f}K_{\text{2h}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mtext>2h</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{m}}\sim\mathcal{U}(\mathcal{f}K_{\text{2h}})</annotation></semantics></math> using 2-hour visibility patterns instead of 4-hour observations.
The out-of-distribution visibilities contain less coverage in high-frequency components, reflecting a slightly more challenging reconstruction task. We test each model on the PROBES test set using the new observation masks. Neither U-LATINO nor RIGAN was fine-tuned to fit masks from <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{f}K_{\text{2h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mtext>2h</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{f}K_{\text{2h}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The remaining methods are zero-shot and designed to operate for any linear Gaussian observation model.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A qualitative comparison for an example out-of distribution reconstruction can be found in <a href="#A2.F10" title="In B.3 Out-of-Distribution Masks for Radio Interferometry ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>; quantitative results averaged over the PROBES dataset can be found in <a href="#A2.T5" title="In B.3 Out-of-Distribution Masks for Radio Interferometry ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Results ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. With the exception of the unfolded model U-LATINO, all methods have a slight reduction in performance compared to <a href="#S4.T2" title="In 4.2.6 Results ‣ 4.2 Radio Interferometry ‣ 4 Numerical Experiments ‣ Deep unfolding of MCMC kernels: scalable, modular &amp; explainable GANs for high-dimensional posterior sampling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. In contrast, the change in forward operators does not affect the performance of U-LATINO, highlighting the resilience of unfolded architectures to subtle changes in the recovery task.</p>
</div>
